                    round_name_r4:          `Stabilise & Present`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the workforce crisis by competency`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Nine experienced RNs are gone in a week — but counting bodies is the wrong lens. The leavers held the <strong>senior charge, code-leadership, wound-care and preceptorship</strong> competencies that supervise, train and keep the unit safe. Coverage on the worst unit has dropped to <strong>62%</strong>, two units have breached the <em>1:4 safe ratio</em>, and four more senior nurses are job-searching. This is a <strong>capability-distribution</strong> problem, not a headcount shortfall — and the agency cap means you can't buy the bodies back even if you wanted to. Name the most safety-critical competency loss, and take the burnout spiral seriously: it turns a one-week shock into a structural collapse.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Redesign roles and models of care`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Rebuild safe care with the talent you have, inside <strong>C$1.4M</strong>, the agency cap, and scope-of-practice rules. A <strong>team-nursing</strong> model with scope optimisation — shifting suitable tasks to allied health, PSWs and unit assistants while RNs lead teams — restores coverage and protects competency transfer through preceptorship. But beware: an efficiency move that <em>strips nurses' autonomy</em> over their own assignments deepens the very burnout you're fighting. Your well-being index is already 41 — squeeze throughput by centralising control and you'll watch the next four senior nurses leave. Test every lever against well-being, not just efficiency.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Decide the AI-adoption stance under a trust constraint`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The network wants a go/no-go on the AI scheduling and decision-support pilot. This is a <strong>leadership and trust</strong> challenge, not a technology purchase — and you're deploying into a burned-out unit that remembers the <em>botched IT rollout two years ago</em>. A <strong>scoped pilot</strong> on one low-risk use-case (scheduling first, not clinical decisions), with full human-in-the-loop guardrails, override-by-default and a named owner for AI errors, builds trust. And the ~40 min/shift it saves is a signal: <strong>reinvest it</strong> in patients and in cutting overtime, and staff see relief; <em>extract it as headcount cuts</em>, and you confirm the union's fear that AI is a layoff tool.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Stabilise, sustain & present to the CEO`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Integrate role redesign, talent allocation and the AI stance into one coherent <strong>90-day plan</strong>, sequenced so you stabilise competency and well-being first, then build. Put a <strong>balanced dashboard</strong> in front of the CEO — leading indicators (well-being, overtime, turnover-intent) <em>and</em> lagging outcomes (safe-ratio, throughput, coverage) — because a throughput-only scorecard wins the quarter and loses the workforce. Then deliver an honest pitch: name the trade-off you accepted and what you chose <strong>not</strong> to do. Treat well-being and retention as hard outcomes, and field the CEO's challenge with credible leading indicators.`,

                    r1_diag_label:          `How do you frame the crisis to your leadership team?`,
                    r1_critical_label:      `Which competency loss is the most safety-critical to flag first?`,
                    r1_cliff_label:         `How seriously do you treat the retention cliff and burnout spiral right now? (%)`,
                    r1_cliff_readout:       `Priority on holding the next 4 senior nurses:`,
                    r1_cliff_hint:          `4 more senior nurses are job-searching; each experienced-RN replacement costs ~C$65,000 and 4–6 months to competency. Overtime is already up 38%.`,
                    diag_competency:        `A capability-distribution problem — specific senior/charge competencies are gone; rebuild the distribution, not the bodies`,
                    diag_headcount:         `A headcount shortfall — we're 9 nurses short; the fix is to find 9 bodies, agency or otherwise`,
                    diag_ratio:             `A ratio problem — two units breached 1:4; spread the remaining nurses thinner to hold the ratio on paper`,
                    diag_morale:            `A morale problem — staff are tired; a thank-you campaign and pizza nights will steady the ship`,
                    crit_seniority:         `Senior charge / code-leadership, wound care and preceptorship — the supervision and competency-transfer backbone`,
                    crit_generic:           `General nursing hours — any RN replaces any other; the gap is just total hours`,
                    crit_documentation:     `Charting and documentation speed — the leavers were fast at notes`,
                    crit_none:              `No single gap is critical — coverage is roughly even across all competencies`,

                    r2_model_label:         `Choose the model-of-care redesign that restores safe coverage.`,
                    r2_skill_label:         `Allocate the C$1.4M in-year flexibility across the skill-mix levers (must total C$1.40M)`,
                    r2_total_readout:       `Stabilisation budget allocated:`,
                    r2_total_cap:           `/ C$1.40M`,
                    r2_autonomy_label:      `Does the redesign protect nurses' control over their own assignments (autonomy)?`,
                    model_team:             `Team nursing + scope optimisation — RNs lead teams, shift suitable tasks to allied health / PSWs / unit assistants, protect autonomy`,
                    model_squeeze:          `Throughput squeeze — raise each nurse's patient load above 1:4 and centralise all assignments to managers`,
                    model_agency:           `Hold for agency — keep the current model and wait for the agency cap to be lifted`,
                    model_float:            `Cross-unit float pool only — pool the remaining nurses and move them wherever the gap is each shift`,
                    alloc_precept:          `Preceptorship & competency transfer — protect the senior-to-junior pipeline`,
                    alloc_allied:           `Allied health / PSW & unit-assistant scope optimisation — offload non-RN tasks safely`,
                    alloc_retain:           `Retention incentives & well-being — protect autonomy, hold the next 4 senior nurses`,
                    auto_protect:           `Yes — build self-scheduling and team-level control into the model; autonomy is a burnout safeguard`,
                    auto_partial:           `Partly — keep some self-scheduling where it's convenient, override it when throughput demands`,
                    auto_strip:             `No — centralise all scheduling and assignments to managers; efficiency comes first`,

                    r3_stance_label:        `Decide the AI-adoption stance for the scheduling / decision-support pilot.`,
                    r3_usecase_label:       `If proceeding, which function do you deploy first?`,
                    r3_guardrails_label:    `Human-in-the-loop, override and accountability design.`,
                    r3_dividend_label:      `Where do the ~40 min/shift of AI time-savings go?`,
                    stance_scoped:          `Scoped pilot — start with one low-risk use-case on one willing unit, with guardrails, then expand on evidence`,
                    stance_fullgo:          `Full go — roll out AI scheduling and clinical decision support unit-wide next week to fix the crisis fast`,
                    stance_defer:           `Defer — postpone the pilot indefinitely to keep the peace with frontline staff`,
                    stance_no:              `No — reject the AI pilot outright; stabilise with no new technology`,
                    uc_scheduling:          `Scheduling optimisation — lower-risk, relieves the rota pressure, easy to keep humans in control`,
                    uc_clinical:            `Clinical decision support — let the AI advise on patient care decisions from day one`,
                    uc_none:                `Neither — we are not deploying any AI function`,
                    guard_full:             `Full guardrails — staff override by default, transparent logic, a named accountable owner for AI errors`,
                    guard_vague:            `Light touch — a policy says humans are responsible, but no override workflow or error owner is named`,
                    guard_autopilot:        `Let it run — trust the algorithm; manual override only if someone complains`,
                    div_reinvest:           `Reinvest it — give the time back to patients and to cutting the overtime that's burning people out`,
                    div_vague:              `Unstated — capture the savings but don't commit publicly where they go`,
                    div_extract:            `Extract it — book the time-savings as a headcount reduction to ease the budget`,

