                    warn_stance_missing:    `Choose an AI-adoption stance before committing.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_competency:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> You named it as a capability-distribution problem, not a body count. The agency cap is maxed and agency nurses don't carry the charge, preceptor and code-leadership competencies you actually lost. Now rebuild that distribution — preceptorship, scope optimisation and team design — rather than chasing bodies you can't have.`,
                    coach_r1_headcount:     `<strong>You've built the plan around finding 9 bodies.</strong> The agency cap is maxed — that door is closed — and even if it weren't, agency nurses don't carry the charge, preceptor and code-leadership competencies you lost. This is a capability-distribution problem. Which specific competencies are missing, and how do you rebuild them without the bodies you can't have?`,
                    coach_r1_ratio:         `<strong>Spreading the remaining nurses thinner holds the ratio on paper and breaks it in practice.</strong> You're not solving the gap, you're hiding it — and you've just stripped supervision from the units that lost their senior nurses. The 1:4 ratio is a safety floor, not a spreadsheet target.`,
                    coach_r1_morale:        `<strong>Pizza nights don't rebuild a competency.</strong> Burnout is real and well-being matters — but the danger here is that you've lost the senior, charge and preceptor capability that keeps the unit safe. Name the capability gap first; the morale work supports the redesign, it doesn't replace it.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Capability-distribution framing AND you flagged the senior charge / code-leadership / preceptorship loss as the safety-critical one. That is exactly the competency-vs-headcount distinction — supervision and competency transfer are what you actually lost, and what your redesign must rebuild.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“Roughly even” is the diagnosis that lets the crisis hide.</strong> The leavers held disproportionate senior and charge competencies — that's why coverage on the worst unit is 62%, not because total hours fell evenly. If you can't name the critical gap, your redesign will rebuild the wrong things.`,
                    coach_r1_cliffignored:  `<strong>You're treating the retention cliff as background noise.</strong> Four more senior nurses are actively job-searching, overtime is up 38%, and each replacement costs ~C$65,000 and months to competency. Ignore the spiral now and Round 2 starts from a worse place — burnout drives turnover drives more burnout.`,
                    coach_r2_team:          `<strong>Team nursing with scope optimisation is the redesign that fits the constraint.</strong> RNs lead teams, suitable tasks move to allied health and PSWs, and preceptorship protects competency transfer despite the senior losses — all inside the cap. Now make sure you funded preceptorship and retention, and protect autonomy.`,
                    coach_r2_squeeze:       `<strong>Your redesign squeezes more throughput per nurse and centralises every assignment.</strong> Efficient on paper — but your well-being index is already 41 and you've just removed the autonomy that protects against burnout. Watch the next four senior nurses leave. Which of your efficiency moves is actually buying you a deeper hole?`,
                    coach_r2_agency:        `<strong>“Wait for the cap to lift” is not a plan.</strong> The cap is a network cost control — it isn't lifting in five days, and your units are below the safe ratio now. Holding the current model with fewer senior nurses just runs everyone on overtime until more of them leave.`,
                    coach_r2_float:         `<strong>A float pool alone moves the gap around; it doesn't close it.</strong> Pooling the remaining nurses and reassigning them every shift erodes continuity and the autonomy that protects well-being. Pair it with team design and scope optimisation, or you're just rotating the burnout.`,
                    coach_r2_noprecept:     `<strong>You funded almost nothing for preceptorship.</strong> The senior nurses you lost were the ones who transferred competency to juniors — starve that pipeline and your coverage gap becomes permanent. Protecting competency transfer is the whole point of redesigning around the losses.`,
                    coach_r2_stripautonomy: `<strong>Good model, wrong control design.</strong> Team nursing only protects well-being if nurses keep a say over their own assignments. Centralise every schedule to managers and you strip the autonomy that is your burnout safeguard — the redesign that should relieve the spiral now feeds it.`,
                    coach_r3_scoped:        `<strong>A scoped pilot is the trust-aware stance.</strong> One low-risk use-case on a willing unit, with guardrails, lets you build evidence and credibility before expanding — the opposite of the last rollout. Now make the guardrails real and tell staff where the time-savings go.`,
                    coach_r3_fullgo:        `<strong>You've green-lit a full AI rollout next week into a burned-out, distrustful unit.</strong> Sriharan's research says AI in healthcare succeeds or fails on leadership and workforce trust, not the algorithm. How is this not the botched rollout, version two — and where are your human-in-the-loop guardrails?`,
                    coach_r3_defer:         `<strong>You've deferred the pilot to keep the peace.</strong> Defensible — but the network offered it precisely because it could relieve the workload crushing your staff, and you're stabilising with no new capacity at all. Is “later” a considered stance with a credible alternative, or just conflict-avoidance?`,
                    coach_r3_no:            `<strong>Rejecting AI outright is also a decision that needs a justification.</strong> “No” keeps the peace today and forgoes the workload relief the tool could have given burned-out staff. Defend it: what's your credible alternative for the capacity you just turned down?`,
                    coach_r3_clinicalfirst: `<strong>Leading with clinical decision support is the high-risk door.</strong> Letting AI advise on patient care from day one in a distrustful unit invites exactly the safety and trust backlash you can least afford. Start with scheduling — lower risk, real relief, and humans stay obviously in control.`,
                    coach_r3_noguardrails:  `<strong>A scoped pilot with no guardrails isn't scoped — it's the same risk in a smaller box.</strong> Without override-by-default, transparent logic and a named owner for AI errors, the first mistake lands on a nurse who never trusted it. Responsible adoption is the guardrails, not the size of the rollout.`,
                    coach_r3_extract:       `<strong>Your AI saves nurses 40 minutes a shift — and you've booked it as a headcount cut.</strong> Tell the union rep that. You've just confirmed their fear that AI is a layoff tool, and you'll get resistance, not adoption. What if those 40 minutes went back to patients or to cutting the overtime that's burning people out?`,
                    coach_r4_stabilise:     `<strong>Sequencing to stabilise first is the right order.</strong> Protect competency and well-being, redesign care, then scope AI onto a base that's recovering rather than collapsing. That's how acute pressure becomes durable redesign instead of temporary firefighting.`,
                    coach_r4_aifirst:       `<strong>Leading with the AI rollout puts the riskiest lever first.</strong> Into a unit that's still below the safe ratio and at 41 well-being, a technology-first sequence repeats the rollout mistake and fits the people problem around the tool. Stabilise the workforce first; the tool serves the plan, not the reverse.`,
                    coach_r4_throughputseq: `<strong>Throughput-first maximises occupancy on a workforce that's already breaking.</strong> You'll hit the number this quarter and lose four more senior nurses next. Well-being and the AI stance aren't “later” items — they're the mechanism that decides whether the throughput holds at all.`,
                    coach_r4_balanced:      `<strong>A balanced dashboard is what proves recovery, not just production.</strong> Leading indicators — well-being, overtime, turnover-intent — tell you the workforce is healing before the lagging safe-ratio and throughput numbers do. That's the dashboard that catches the spiral early.`,
                    coach_r4_throughputdash: `<strong>Your dashboard tracks occupancy and wait times — and nothing about well-being or turnover-intent.</strong> You'll hit the throughput target for one quarter and lose four more senior nurses. What leading indicator tells you the workforce is recovering, not just producing?`,
                    coach_r4_rosy:          `<strong>Promising full recovery in a quarter with no trade-offs isn't a plan, it's a hostage to fortune.</strong> The CEO needs to know what you accepted and what you chose not to do — a credible plan names its trade-offs. The rosy version unravels the first time a leading indicator slips.`,
                    coach_r4_ceowin:        `<strong>That's the integrated plan the CEO can stand behind.</strong> Stabilise-first sequencing, a balanced leading/lagging dashboard, and an honest pitch that names the trade-off — competency rebuilt, well-being treated as a hard outcome, and AI adopted on trust. That's how a one-week shock becomes durable redesign.`,

