                    insight_resilient:      `Stabilised & recovering: you solved the crisis as a competency-distribution problem — team nursing that protected autonomy, preceptorship that rebuilt the senior pipeline, a scoped AI pilot whose dividend went back to staff, and a dashboard that tracks recovery. Coverage is safe, well-being is climbing past the target, and the workforce is healing, not just producing.`,
                    insight_burnout:        `Burnout spiral deepened: an efficiency move that stripped nurses' autonomy bought throughput on paper and drove the well-being index down. The mechanism that turned a one-week shock into a structural risk — burnout drives turnover drives overtime drives burnout — is now accelerating, not interrupted.`,
                    insight_extracted:      `Trust broken: booking the AI time-savings as a headcount cut confirmed the union's fear that AI is a layoff tool. You got resistance, not adoption — the dividend you extracted cost you the trust the whole pilot depended on.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully integrate the three levers. Competency rebuild, the care redesign and the AI stance still pull in slightly different directions — enough that the stabilisation holds for now but the spiral isn't decisively broken.`,
                    insight_agency:         `You leaned on agency / headcount thinking. The cap is maxed and agency nurses don't carry the senior competencies you lost — the bodies-not-capability instinct kept resurfacing and slowed the real rebuild.`,
                    insight_throughput:     `Your scorecard tilted to throughput and cost over well-being and turnover-intent. You may hit the occupancy number this quarter, but the leading indicators that warn of the next wave of resignations aren't on the board.`,
                    insight_silverbullet:   `You treated AI as a fix for the staffing problem rather than a leadership-and-trust challenge. Rushing it into a distrustful unit without guardrails risked repeating the botched rollout — the algorithm was never the hard part.`,
                    insight_wellbeing:      `Well-being is past the network target and trust is high — you treated them as hard outcomes, not nice-to-haves. That is the leading evidence the workforce is recovering, which is what actually sustains safe capacity and throughput.`,

                    hint_extract:           `You've booked the AI time-savings as headcount cuts — that's the move that confirms the union's fears and kills adoption. Reinvest the dividend in patients and overtime relief, or trust collapses.`,
                    hint_burnout:           `Your well-being index is falling — an efficiency move has stripped autonomy and is feeding the burnout spiral. Restore nurses' control over their assignments and fund retention before the next senior nurse leaves.`,
                    hint_agency:            `You keep reaching for bodies. The agency cap is maxed and agency nurses lack the senior competencies you lost — rebuild capability through preceptorship, scope optimisation and team design instead.`,
                    hint_coverage:          `Safe coverage is still below 70%. Protect competency transfer (preceptorship) and a team-nursing model that uses allied-health scope — that's how you rebuild capability without the bodies you can't hire.`,
                    hint_trust:             `Staff trust is dangerously low — the AI stance and where the dividend goes are driving it. Scope the pilot, build real guardrails, and reinvest the time-savings visibly in staff.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — coverage safe, well-being past target, and high trust. Lock in the stabilise-first plan and a balanced leading/lagging dashboard, and pitch the CEO honestly to hold it.`,

